WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:02.880
- [Narrator] If you want
to buy this pair of Adidas,

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.740
you can use your credit card

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.200
or a buy now, pay later service.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.450
The result seems similar.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.300
You don't pay upfront, but with BNPL,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.210
you can break that full
payment out into installments

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.220
usually with no interest.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.360
Buy now, pay later firms

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.700
have boomed over the last three years

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.980
and companies like PayPal, Apple,

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.730
and credit cards themselves
are joining in on the hype.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.770
But as some of the companies scale up

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.570
with their growing customer base,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.730
they're struggling to turn a profit.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.460
Here's how buy now, pay
later services compare

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.410
to credit cards and what it means

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.690
for the next time you're checking out.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.220
- Everything today is pay-as-you-go.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.740
- [Narrator] Dan Dolev is an
analyst who follows companies

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.320
like Affirm, Block and PayPal.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.600
- Credit is becoming a pay-as-you-go thing

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.870
especially in very young
millennial demographics.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.540
- [Narrator] Buy now, pay later
companies took off in the US

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.770
during the pandemic.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.550
Affirm, the only BNPL
focused company that's public

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.730
in the US reported its
revenue more than doubled

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.290
over the last two years.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.600
One of the main ways
these companies make money

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.760
is through merchant discount rates.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.110
A fee the retailer pays

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.930
to the company on each buy
now, pay later purchase.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.580
- Selling is better than not selling

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.680
and merchants are willing
to accept somewhat

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.780
of a discount in order to make the sale.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.540
- [Narrator] The number of
retailers signed on to use Affirm

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.917
jumped from just 6,000 in
2020 to 235,000 in 2022.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:28.643
Part of the reason

00:01:28.643 --> 00:01:31.230
for its popularity is
that buy now, pay later

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.330
is more accessible than a credit card.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.800
- BNPL is very attractive

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.110
to people who have credit difficulties.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.580
- [Narrator] Todd Baker is a lecturer

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.520
at Columbia Law School
who's worked as an executive

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.460
in finance and teaches
about emerging FinTech.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.900
- Other people do it just because
it seems like a good idea.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.180
- [Narrator] Let's go back
to that cart with our Adidas.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.320
with a credit card,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.480
You need approval to use that credit

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.210
often well before you put
your shoes in the cart.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.970
Companies like Affirm, Klarna and Afterpay

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.700
on the other hand, can approve on the spot

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.190
and make approvals purchase by purchase.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.740
That's because of the different
ways they check your credit.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.720
Credit card companies do a deep dive

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.930
into your credit history that
shows up on your credit score.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.340
Buy now, pay later companies

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.190
typically perform a
less formal credit check

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.870
and look at additional data

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.480
like previous times
you've used their service.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.730
BNPL can give more spending power

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.260
to people who may not have access

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.650
to credit or have credit built
up, but that can be an issue.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.560
- There is a risk that credit
will be extended to you

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.560
in a situation where you're
not likely to be able to repay

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:35.730
and that's not good either

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.720
for the issuer of the
credit or for the consumer.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.580
- [Narrator] Affirm's CEO Max Levchin

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.500
says the company accounts for this.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.520
- We do have to say no

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.800
and tell our consumers
with love and compassion

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.000
you're overextending yourself.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.960
Please don't buy this.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.880
- [Narrator] Klarna says
it also takes measures

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.650
to make sure it only extends loans

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.510
to borrowers who can repay.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.660
Both Klarna and Afterpay
restrict use of their services

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.640
if a borrower misses a payment.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.670
Buy now, pay later lacks some
of the regulated safeguards

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.370
that credit cards have
for consumers in the US.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.290
That's in part because they're not covered

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.150
by the Truth In Lending Act.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.880
This is a law that requires
that lenders provide you all

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.630
of your loan information
upfront, no hidden costs.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.550
It applies to loans that are paid for

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.530
in more than four installments.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.080
Buy now, pay later falls just below that.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.120
- The product was structured essentially

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.450
to avoid the application of
the Truth and Lending Act.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.820
The beauty of BNPL for the retailers

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.950
that it's instantaneous

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.230
and the provision of
significant disclosure

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.840
before a particular transaction
would be very difficult.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.307
- [Narrator] Affirm
says it provides a truth

00:03:38.307 --> 00:03:41.310
and lending disclosure
for all credit extensions

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.800
and Klarna says it supports
additional regulation

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.060
of the sector.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.090
The Consumer Financial
Protection Bureau also warns

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.640
that consumers could risk over extension

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.070
meaning they have more
debt than they can handle.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.110
This could happen
because they could borrow

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.120
from multiple lenders at once

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.030
since BNPL platforms can't
see borrowing activity

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.830
on other platforms.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.140
Customer defaults, when they happen,

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.480
can have an impact on company's costs.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.000
So they have to balance
the risk of delinquencies

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.240
with approving enough
transactions to cover their costs.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.850
- That's the key to a
successful buy now, pay later.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:18.240
It's underwriting,
underwriting, underwriting.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.460
- [Narrator] Klarna said that
its underwriting practices

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.670
have helped keep its
default rates below 1%,

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.470
but delinquencies aren't the only thing

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.570
that can undermine profits.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.990
Shifting consumer habits
can cut down on transactions

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:34.200
and many buy now, pay later
programs rely on funding

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.420
that is sensitive to interest rates.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.240
Since merchant fees make up
the bulk of their profit,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.430
companies might have to
raise them to make up

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.290
for higher borrowing costs.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.140
- If interest rates go up a
lot, then they're in trouble

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.720
because the cost of
money's gonna be too high

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.200
to more than offset with
the merchant discount rate.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.560
- [Narrator] Affirm and Klarna
offer services beyond BNPL

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.740
and are continuing to
diversify their offerings

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.050
but they're also facing
increasing competition

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.740
from Apple, PayPal and credit
card installment plans.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.620
Still, for some buy now, pay later CEOs

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.260
like Affirm's Levchin,
the competition is clear.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.540
- The enemy or the competitor
is the credit cards,

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:13.373
not each other.

00:05:13.373 --> 00:05:14.970
- [Narrator] Despite these challenges,

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.760
Delev thinks that buy now,
pay later is here to stay.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.000
- And as they become bigger
and offer more services,

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.330
they will be able to gain scale
and increase profitability.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.330
- [Narrator] But some buy now,
pay later firms like Afterpay

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.250
have been bought by larger companies.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.020
- The fundamental
question has always been,

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.850
is BNPL a business

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.796
or is it just a product
for other providers

00:05:35.796 --> 00:05:37.290
of financial services?

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:40.350
And I think the consensus
seems to be shifting

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.570
towards the idea that BNPL

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:45.510
as a standalone business is
not likely to be successful

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:46.963
in the competitive market.

00:05:46.963 --> 00:05:49.546
(gentle music)

